 - a Switch 2 game's frame in its heavy scenes is dominated by one compute dispatch between the render-resolution passes and the output-resolution ones: Donkey Kong Bananza's upscaler takes 3.5-5 ms of a 24 ms GPU frame on an M1 in handheld mode (960x540 → 1920x1080, `DM_NVN_GPUPROF`), and the game cannot reach its 60 fps interval there. → [A game's own upscaler drawn by MetalFX](../reference/rendering.md#a-games-own-upscaler-drawn-by-metalfx)
+- Ocarina of Time's title movie shows rectangular colour blocks while the logo, menu and subtitles remain legible. → [Block corruption after NDVE movie reconstruction](../reference/rendering.md#block-corruption-after-ndve-movie-reconstruction)
 - with `DM_UPSCALER=metalfx`, a game's picture is washed out wherever it upscales: lifted shadows and low contrast, the scene only, with the UI drawn after it correct; with `DM_UPSCALER=game`, or at a size the game does not upscale, it is right. → [An upscaler's colour read from its code](../reference/rendering.md#an-upscalers-colour-read-from-its-code)
 - tall blue / textured panels that flickered frame to frame: the fountain's vertex shader pushed the water surface to the top of the screen. → [Metal 4 does not order operations inside one encoder](../reference/rendering.md#metal-4-does-not-order-operations-inside-one-encoder)
 - a buffer copy, inline update or fill completes, but a following copy through another Metal buffer over the same allocation reads stale bytes. → [Buffer copies through aliased Metal addresses](../reference/rendering.md#buffer-copies-through-aliased-metal-addresses)
